<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<assembly manifestVersion="1.0" xmlns="urn:schemas-microsoft-com:asm.v1">
  <assemblyIdentity version="1.0.0.0" name="Strata" />

  <trustInfo xmlns="urn:schemas-microsoft-com:asm.v2">
    <security>
      <requestedPrivileges xmlns="urn:schemas-microsoft-com:asm.v3">
        <!--
          Strata runs unelevated.

          It does not need elevation for its ordinary work: reading the NTFS change journal only
          requires a directory handle on the volume root plus FSCTL_READ_UNPRIVILEGED_USN_JOURNAL,
          which any user has. Verified end to end on this machine.

          Two things are given up by not elevating, both acceptable:

            1. Journal records arrive with their file names redacted, so a path has to be resolved
               from the file ID per changed file. Measured at 0.06 ms against the 0.09 ms size read
               it accompanies, so this is a rounding error rather than a cost.
            2. A baseline cannot read ACL'd directories. Unelevated, 557 directories on C: were
               unreadable and the volume total came out 29.6 GB short of what Windows reports. The
               scan counts and reports them, so the shortfall is visible rather than silent.

          One operation still requires administrator: FSCTL_CREATE_USN_JOURNAL, needed once per
          volume on volumes where Windows has not already enabled a journal. That is what
          'strata enable-journal' is for, and it is a one-time manual step from an elevated prompt
          rather than a reason to elevate the resident process.

          Staying asInvoker also means auto-start is a plain scheduled task or Run key entry with no
          UAC prompt at login. See docs/RUNTIME.md.
        -->
        <requestedExecutionLevel level="asInvoker" uiAccess="false" />
      </requestedPrivileges>
    </security>
  </trustInfo>

  <compatibility xmlns="urn:schemas-microsoft-com:compatibility.v1">
    <application>
      <!-- Windows 10 / 11 -->
      <supportedOS Id="{8e0f7a12-bfb3-4fe8-b9a5-48fd50a15a9a}" />
    </application>
  </compatibility>

  <application xmlns="urn:schemas-microsoft-com:asm.v3">
    <windowsSettings>
      <!-- Paths on this machine routinely exceed MAX_PATH; the scanner also uses \\?\ prefixes. -->
      <longPathAware xmlns="http://schemas.microsoft.com/SMI/2016/WindowsSettings">true</longPathAware>
      <activeCodePage xmlns="http://schemas.microsoft.com/SMI/2019/WindowsSettings">UTF-8</activeCodePage>
    </windowsSettings>
  </application>
</assembly>
